# AH Collection Of Clothes — Full Stack Store

Your site now has a real backend. Here's what changed and how to run it.

## What was added
- *server.js* — Node.js + Express server with API routes:
  - GET /api/products — list products (from data/products.json)
  - GET /api/cart — get current cart (tracked via cookie, no login needed)
  - POST /api/cart/add — add item to cart
  - POST /api/cart/remove — remove item from cart
  - POST /api/checkout — place an order (saves to data/orders.json)
  - POST /api/newsletter — save email signups (data/newsletter.json)
- *public/index.html* — same design as before, but products, cart, and checkout now talk to the backend instead of being fake/static.
- *data/* — JSON files acting as a simple database (products, orders, carts, newsletter emails). Good enough to start; swap for a real database (SQLite/Postgres) later if you grow.

## How to run it locally
1. Install [Node.js](https://nodejs.org) if you don't have it.
2. Open a terminal in this folder and run:
   
   npm install
   npm start
   
3. Open http://localhost:3000 in your browser.

## What's still missing (important)
- *Real payments are not connected.* Checkout right now just records the order as pending_payment — no money actually moves. To take real payments, you'll need a processor like *Stripe* or *PayPal*, which needs a business/merchant account. I can wire this in when you're ready.
- *No admin panel yet* — right now you'd check data/orders.json directly to see orders. I can build a simple admin page to view/manage orders and stock.
- *Not deployed yet* — this runs on your own computer for now. To make it live on the internet, deploy it to something like *Render* or *Railway* (both have free tiers), and I can walk you through that step by step.

## Notes
- Cart is tied to a browser cookie, so each visitor gets their own cart automatically.
- Stock numbers in data/products.json are for display only right now — they don't yet auto-decrease when someone orders. Say the word and I'll add that.
